1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,673
She has been awake since two in the morning.
The candle is nearly gone.

2
00:00:04,673 --> 00:00:09,347
It is the nineteenth of May, fifteen
thirty-six. The Tower is quiet.

3
00:00:09,347 --> 00:00:14,020
Three years ago she was crowned queen, in
a gown of white and gold.

4
00:00:14,020 --> 00:00:18,694
Today she will die by the sword. She
asked for the sword herself.

5
00:00:18,694 --> 00:00:23,367
The king wanted a son. She gave him a
daughter, then nothing at all.

6
00:00:23,367 --> 00:00:28,041
The daughter's name was Elizabeth. No one yet
knew what she would become.

7
00:00:28,041 --> 00:00:32,714
By spring the king had found someone new.
Anne had become a problem.

8
00:00:32,714 --> 00:00:37,387
The charges came fast. Treason. Adultery.
Plots almost no one believed.

9
00:00:37,387 --> 00:00:42,061
Five men were accused with her. All
five were already dead.

10
00:00:42,061 --> 00:00:46,734
They died two days ago. She watched from
her window. She knew.

11
00:00:46,734 --> 00:00:51,408
Now she waits for her own turn. The
waiting is the worst of it.

12
00:00:51,408 --> 00:00:56,081
She was told it would happen at nine. She
prepared herself for nine.

13
00:00:56,081 --> 00:01:00,755
Nine o'clock came. Then it passed.
No one came for her.

14
00:01:00,755 --> 00:01:05,428
The execution was delayed. No reason given.
The waiting began again.

15
00:01:05,428 --> 00:01:10,101
She had said her confession. She had
already made her peace.

16
00:01:10,101 --> 00:01:14,775
Now she had to hold that peace, hour
after hour, and wait.

17
00:01:14,775 --> 00:01:19,448
They had brought a swordsman from
France, famous for his skill.

18
00:01:19,448 --> 00:01:24,122
A sword, not an axe. It was, in
its way, a mercy.

19
00:01:24,122 --> 00:01:28,795
The axe was clumsy. It often took more than
one blow. The sword was clean.

20
00:01:28,795 --> 00:01:33,469
She had asked for it. The king allowed it. A
small kindness, at the end.

21
00:01:33,469 --> 00:01:38,142
The swordsman waited too. Told to
wait, like everyone else.

22
00:01:38,142 --> 00:01:42,815
Outside, the scaffold was being built. She
could hear the hammering.

23
00:01:42,815 --> 00:01:47,489
A low platform, a few feet high. Spread
with straw for the blood.

24
00:01:47,489 --> 00:01:52,162
It was no public spectacle. Only a few
hundred would watch.

25
00:01:52,162 --> 00:01:56,836
A private death, for the most
talked-about woman in England.

26
00:01:56,836 --> 00:02:01,509
Noon approached. Still she waited. The
second delay stretched on.

27
00:02:01,509 --> 00:02:06,183
Those who saw her said she was calm.
Calmer than anyone expected.

28
00:02:06,183 --> 00:02:10,856
She even joked. The executioner was good, she
said, and her neck was small.

29
00:02:10,856 --> 00:02:15,529
She laughed as she said it. No one
knew how to answer.

30
00:02:15,529 --> 00:02:20,203
Her ladies wept. She comforted them. The
queen comforting her servants.

31
00:02:20,203 --> 00:02:24,876
She asked them to pray for her. And to be
kind, when she was gone.

32
00:02:24,876 --> 00:02:29,550
The afternoon faded. The day given to her
to die was nearly over.

33
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:34,223
Then word came at last. It would be the
next morning, at nine.

34
00:02:34,223 --> 00:02:38,897
She had survived the longest day of her
life. One night remained.

35
00:02:38,897 --> 00:02:43,570
She barely slept. She prayed in the
dark with her almoner.

36
00:02:43,570 --> 00:02:48,243
By dawn she was dressed. A grey gown trimmed
with fur. Crimson beneath.

37
00:02:48,243 --> 00:02:52,917
She dressed with care. She would be
looked at, one last time.

38
00:02:52,917 --> 00:02:57,590
A white cap, to hold her hair from her
neck. Chosen with purpose.

39
00:02:57,590 --> 00:03:02,264
She would die looking like a queen,
because she had been one.

40
00:03:02,264 --> 00:03:06,937
At nine they came. This time, no one
turned them away.

41
00:03:06,937 --> 00:03:11,611
She walked out into the morning, surrounded by
those who would witness it.

42
00:03:11,611 --> 00:03:16,284
The green was bright. The grass wet with
dew. The crowd silent.

43
00:03:16,284 --> 00:03:20,958
She walked toward the scaffold slowly,
her ladies close behind.

44
00:03:20,958 --> 00:03:25,631
She climbed the few steps. The straw was
pale beneath her feet.

45
00:03:25,631 --> 00:03:30,304
She was allowed to speak. The condemned
could address the crowd.

46
00:03:30,304 --> 00:03:34,978
She did not protest her innocence. She
did not accuse the king.

47
00:03:34,978 --> 00:03:39,651
She spoke gently of him. She called him a
good and gentle prince.

48
00:03:39,651 --> 00:03:44,325
No one believed it. She knew it too.
She said it anyway.

49
00:03:44,325 --> 00:03:48,998
Why? Perhaps for the daughter she left behind.
Words could still wound a king.

50
00:03:48,998 --> 00:03:53,672
She asked the crowd to pray for her.
Then her words were done.

51
00:03:53,672 --> 00:03:58,345
Her ladies came to help her. Their
hands shook. Hers did not.

52
00:03:58,345 --> 00:04:03,018
They took her necklace and headdress, so
nothing would be in the way.

53
00:04:03,018 --> 00:04:07,692
She tucked her hair beneath a plain cap.
Calm, practical, to the last.

54
00:04:07,692 --> 00:04:12,365
She gave her ladies a last look. She
told them not to grieve.

55
00:04:12,365 --> 00:04:17,039
Then she knelt upright in the straw. There
was no block for the sword.

56
00:04:17,039 --> 00:04:21,712
A blindfold was tied gently over her
eyes. The world went dark.

57
00:04:21,712 --> 00:04:26,386
She prayed aloud. God, have pity on my
soul. Again and again.

58
00:04:26,386 --> 00:04:31,059
The swordsman did not want her to flinch.
So he did something quiet.

59
00:04:31,059 --> 00:04:35,732
He called out softly, as if to a
helper. Bring me the sword.

60
00:04:35,732 --> 00:04:40,406
She turned her head toward the sound,
away from the blade.

61
00:04:40,406 --> 00:04:45,079
The sword was already in his hands. She
never knew the moment came.

62
00:04:45,079 --> 00:04:49,753
It was over in a single stroke.
Clean, as she had hoped.

63
00:04:49,753 --> 00:04:54,426
The crowd made no sound. Many knelt.
Some were weeping.

64
00:04:54,426 --> 00:04:59,100
No coffin was ready for her. No one had
thought to prepare one.

65
00:04:59,100 --> 00:05:03,773
Her ladies wrapped her themselves
and carried her away.

66
00:05:03,773 --> 00:05:08,446
They found an old arrow chest. It was
the only box that fit.

67
00:05:08,446 --> 00:05:13,120
The most famous woman in England, buried
in a box made for arrows.

68
00:05:13,120 --> 00:05:17,793
She was laid beneath the stones of the
chapel, inside the Tower walls.

69
00:05:17,793 --> 00:05:22,467
No monument. No name. For centuries,
almost nothing marked her.

70
00:05:22,467 --> 00:05:27,140
The king was dressed for hunting. He
married eleven days later.

71
00:05:27,140 --> 00:05:31,814
Her name was struck from the records. Her
badges torn from the walls.

72
00:05:31,814 --> 00:05:36,487
As if England had been ordered to
forget she ever lived.

73
00:05:36,487 --> 00:05:41,160
But she had left something behind. A
red-haired daughter, not yet three.

74
00:05:41,160 --> 00:05:45,834
That daughter was declared illegitimate. Pushed
aside. Almost forgotten too.

75
00:05:45,834 --> 00:05:50,507
And then, against everything, that
forgotten girl became a queen.

76
00:05:50,507 --> 00:05:55,181
Elizabeth the First. One of the greatest
rulers England has known.

77
00:05:55,181 --> 00:05:59,854
She ruled forty-four years. She never
married. She answered to no man.

78
00:05:59,854 --> 00:06:04,528
She rarely spoke of her mother. But she
wore a ring all her life.

79
00:06:04,528 --> 00:06:09,201
Inside the ring was a hidden portrait. A
woman with dark eyes.

80
00:06:09,201 --> 00:06:13,874
It was Anne. The daughter had carried
her, hidden, all along.

81
00:06:13,874 --> 00:06:18,548
The king tried to erase her. His own
daughter made him fail.

82
00:06:18,548 --> 00:06:23,221
Anne Boleyn waited through the longest
hours a person can wait.

83
00:06:23,221 --> 00:06:27,895
And she met her end the way she
lived. On her own terms.

84
00:06:27,895 --> 00:06:32,568
They gave her one day to die. She made it
last five hundred years.
